WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.069
We are now hearing from more and more politicians
that they want a $15 minimum wage.

00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:08.660
Wouldn't this cause inflation?

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:13.870
So here's what you have to understand about
a question like the $15 minimum wage.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:22.029
Um, every policy that is put in place has
um, items on both sides of the ledger.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:24.499
We've said wouldn't Medicare for all killed
jobs?

00:00:24.499 --> 00:00:26.369
Yes, it would kill some jobs.

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:27.869
It would create others.

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:28.869
It would increase.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:34.000
Uh, the, the, the net benefit I believe is
very clear for Medicare for all, but it would

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.520
kill some jobs and that's not a reason not
to do it.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.080
That's a reason to figure out the way to do
it the right way.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:45.650
If you talk about increasing the minimum wage,
minimum wage increases to some degree can

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.760
accelerate price inflation.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.730
The problem is that that is a talking point
being used by the right to just oppose even

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:55.060
common sense increases to the minimum wage.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.610
The minimum wage has fallen way behind inflation.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.450
It has fallen way behind productivity.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:04.049
There is no index that justifies as low a
minimum wage as that which we have now.

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:08.750
And the reality is that labor costs are only
part of a businesses total costs.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:12.790
So there have been studies done which suggest
if McDonald's, I don't remember the study

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:19.570
exactly, but the idea was if McDonald's goes
to a whatever, minimum wage, 12 $15, whatever,

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.870
that would be a significant raise for a lot
of employees.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:29.300
But you could match that by increasing the
cost of a big Mac by 10 cents because averaged

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:36.240
into all or that all of that are fixed costs,
rent, food costs, transportation, executive

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.720
salaries, connectivity, and it systems, all
of this different stuff.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.720
So yes, the w when you increase a minimum
wage, there can be an inflationary effect,

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.510
but that doesn't mean we should be scared.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:54.400
If you are increasing the buying power of
so many employees, demand side stimulus, making

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.990
them better able to afford their lives, they
have disposable income to go and spend in

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:03.210
their communities, which has an economic multiplier
effect.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:08.170
The small inflationary effect on the price
of, for example, food at McDonald's in order

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:15.720
to give everybody a meaningful raise is a
very valid and palatable trade to be made.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.670
So when we get these black and white questions
isn't a $15 minimum wage bad because it'll

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:21.950
cause inflation.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:27.090
The question needs to be how much inflation
will it cost and how does that relate to the

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.620
overarching cost of living in these areas?

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:36.760
And that's where I've read increasingly very
good economic analysis, which say $15 minimum

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:42.770
wage is probably too low in some high cost
of living places, but it may be too high in

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:48.460
some other places in low cost of living parts
of the country, uh, with a much lower current

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:54.590
minimum wage, going to 15 could double the
minimum wage that may be out of line with

00:02:54.590 --> 00:03:01.780
what the local economy, uh, needs or what
the local, uh, economic parameters dictate.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:05.780
That's not me being against the $15 minimum
wage and some places it should be 22.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:11.440
When you look at cost of living, this is me
saying that any one size fits all black white

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.580
thinking about this issue is likely to be
wrong.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.319
And we have to look at all of these issues
in more detail.

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:23.879
Any policy has an upside and a downside and
economics.

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:26.280
The science of economics done well.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:33.349
Um, and economic analysis can tell us when
the benefit outweighs the cost for the broader

00:03:33.349 --> 00:03:34.910
betterment of society.

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:36.120
Let me know what you think.

